<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Wallis and Futuna</h1><p class="st-meta">1887 CE – present · capital Mata-Utu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A French Pacific territory of two island groups where three traditional kings still share authority with the French state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-uvea">Wallis</a> was the seat of the Kingdom of Uvea, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavelua">Lavelua</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futuna-wallis-and-futuna">Futuna</a> was divided between the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sigave">Sigave</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-alo">Alo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-mary-marists">Marist</a> missionaries arrived in 1837; on Futuna the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-chanel">Pierre Chanel</a> was killed in 1841 and later canonised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amelia-tokagahahau-aliki">Queen Amelia of Uvea</a> signed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1887, and the Futuna kings followed in 1888.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> was administered from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-caledonia">New Caledonia</a>, and the Catholic Church held great influence over schooling and daily life, while the kings kept customary authority over land and local justice. The islands lived by subsistence farming and fishing. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-uvea">Wallis</a> hosted an American base. Many islanders emigrated to New <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-caledonia-darien">Caledonia</a> for work.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>After a referendum in 1959, Wallis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futuna-wallis-and-futuna">Futuna</a> became a French overseas territory in 1961 and an overseas collectivity in 2003. The three kings remain recognised in its statute, and more Wallisians and Futunans now live in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-caledonia">New Caledonia</a> than at home.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wallis-and-futuna.jpg" alt="Wallis and Futuna" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Wallis_and_Futuna.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1887 CE – present</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mata-Utu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Amelia Tokagahahau Aliki</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Wallisian, Futunan, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wallis-et-Futuna</dd></dl></aside></div>

